## Branch naming convention

Format:
- **`<type>/<short-description-in-kebab-case>`**
- optional: **`<type>/<scope>-<short-description>`**

Allowed `type` (short whitelist):
- **`feat/`** – feature (typically PR `semver:minor`)
- **`fix/`** – bugfix (typically PR `semver:patch`)
- **`ci/`** – pipelines, workflows, caching, release automation
- **`chore/`** – maintenance (deps, tooling, repo housekeeping)
- **`docs/`** – documentation
- **`refactor/`** – refactor w/o behavior change (typically PR `semver:none`)
- **`test/`** – tests only

Style:
- lowercase only, numbers ok
- separator **`-`**
- no diacritics, no spaces, no `_`
- keep it short (≈ 50–70 chars max)

Breaking change:
- do **not** use `!` in branch names
- use `feat/breaking-...` / `refactor/breaking-...` if you want
- breaking is defined in the **PR**: title `feat!:` or `BREAKING CHANGE:` + label `semver:major`

Automation reservation:
- do not use prefix **`release-please--...`** (reserved for release-please)

Parallel agents: one branch per independent task; use git worktrees (see `parallel-worktrees.mdc`). GitHub issue numbers are optional in the name; do not invent GitLab/Jira ticket prefixes.

Examples:
- `feat/obs-scene-autoswitch`
- `fix/obs-connection-timeout`
- `ci/release-please-workflow`
- `docs/release-policy-clarify`
- `refactor/state-machine-cleanup`

